#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:1
msgid "Name"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:3
msgid "`nix.conf` - Nix configuration file"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:5
msgid "Description"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:7
msgid ""
"Nix supports a variety of configuration settings, which are read from "
"configuration files or taken as command line flags."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:9
msgid "Configuration file"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:11
msgid ""
"By default Nix reads settings from each of the following places. Settings "
"are applied on top of one another, so later settings overwrite earlier "
"entries."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:14
msgid ""
"The system-wide configuration file `nix.conf` in the configuration directory."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:16
msgid ""
"The configuration directory defaults to `${sysconfdir}/nix` (i.e. `/etc/nix` "
"on most Unix systems) or `%PROGRAMDATA%\\nix\\conf` on Windows, and can be "
"overridden with [`NIX_CONF_DIR`](./env-common.md#env-NIX_CONF_DIR)."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:18
msgid ""
"Values loaded in this file are not forwarded to the Nix daemon. The client "
"assumes that the daemon has already loaded them."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:21
msgid ""
"If [`NIX_USER_CONF_FILES`](./env-common.md#env-NIX_USER_CONF_FILES) is set, "
"then each path in the list will be loaded in reverse order. The list "
"separator is `:` on Unix and `;` on Windows."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:24
msgid "Otherwise it will look for `nix.conf` in:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:26
msgid "The [user configuration directory](./env-common.md#user-conf-dir)"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:27
msgid ""
"On Unix additionally, `nix/nix.conf` under each directory in "
"[`XDG_CONFIG_DIRS`](./env-common.md#env-XDG_CONFIG_DIRS)"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:29
msgid ""
"If [`NIX_CONFIG`](./env-common.md#env-NIX_CONFIG) is set, its contents are "
"treated as the contents of a configuration file. In particular, settings are "
"separated by newlines, just as in the configuration file."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:32
msgid "File format"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:34
msgid ""
"Configuration files consist of `name = value` pairs, one per line. Comments "
"start with a `#` character."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:37 source/command-ref/conf-file.md:749
msgid "Example:"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:44
msgid ""
"Other files can be included with a line like `include <path>`, where "
"`<path>` is interpreted relative to the current configuration file. A "
"missing file is an error unless `!include` is used instead."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:47
msgid ""
"A configuration setting usually overrides any previous value. However, for "
"settings that take a list of items, you can prefix the name of the setting "
"by `extra-` to _append_ to the previous value."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:50
msgid "For instance,"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:57
msgid "defines the `substituters` setting to be `a b c d`."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:59
msgid ""
"Unknown option names are not an error, and are simply ignored with a warning."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:61
msgid "Command line flags"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:63
msgid ""
"Configuration options can be set on the command line, overriding the values "
"set in the [configuration file](#configuration-file):"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:65
msgid ""
"Every configuration setting has corresponding command line flag (e.g. `--max-"
"jobs 16`). Boolean settings do not need an argument, and can be explicitly "
"disabled with the `no-` prefix (e.g. `--keep-failed` and `--no-keep-failed`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:68
msgid ""
"Unknown option names are invalid flags (unless there is already a flag with "
"that name), and are rejected with an error."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:70
msgid ""
"The flag `--option <name> <value>` is interpreted exactly like a `<name> = "
"<value>` in a setting file."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:72
msgid "Unknown option names are ignored with a warning."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:74
msgid ""
"The `extra-` prefix is supported for settings that take a list of items (e."
"g. `--extra-trusted users alice` or `--option extra-trusted-users alice`)."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:76
msgid "Integer settings"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:78
msgid ""
"Settings that have an integer type support the suffixes `K`, `M`, `G` and "
"`T`. These cause the specified value to be multiplied by 2^10, 2^20, 2^30 "
"and 2^40, respectively. For instance, `--min-free 1M` is equivalent to `--"
"min-free 1048576`."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:83
msgid "Available settings"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:85
msgid ""
"<span id=\"conf-abort-on-warn\">[`abort-on-warn`](#conf-abort-on-warn)</span>"
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:87
msgid ""
"If set to true, [`builtins.warn`](../language/builtins.md#builtins-warn) "
"throws an error when logging a warning."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:89
msgid ""
"This will give you a stack trace that leads to the location of the warning."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:91
msgid ""
"This is useful for finding information about warnings in third-party Nix "
"code when you can not start the interactive debugger, such as when Nix is "
"called from a non-interactive script. See [`debugger-on-warn`](#conf-"
"debugger-on-warn)."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:93
msgid ""
"Currently, a stack trace can only be produced when the debugger is enabled, "
"or when evaluation is aborted."
msgstr ""

#: source/command-ref/conf-file.md:95
msgid ""
"This option can be enabled by setting `NIX_ABORT_ON_WARN=1` in the "
"environment."
msgstr ""

